scipy: call BLAS and LAPACK with OpenBLAS's C signatures.

OpenBLAS's C LAPACK and its BLAS interface define Fortran subroutines as
int functions (as f2c makes them), without hidden string lengths, and
numpy declares them so. scipy declares them void, directly and in the
f2py modules it generates (_fblas, _flapack), and passes a length to
dlagtm. Native ABIs ignore that, but wasm doesn't: a call through the
wrong signature traps. So the declarations, and scipy's *wrp wrappers
that f2py calls the same way, are int. Its public Cython API keeps its
signatures, except that cython_lapack leaves out chla_transtype and
xerbla_array, whose f2c signatures differ from Fortran's. (Pyodide's
scipy makes the same int change.)

--- a/scipy/_build_utils/src/_blas64_defines.h
+++ b/scipy/_build_utils/src/_blas64_defines.h
@@ -29,7 +29,7 @@
  *
  * As a net result, the f2py-generated C code of the sort
  *
- * extern void F_FUNC(srotg,SROTG)(float*,float*,float*,float* );
+ * extern int F_FUNC(srotg,SROTG)(float*,float*,float*,float* );
  *
  * will in fact be `BLAS_FUNC(srotg)(...)` --- which accounts for the ILP64 name mangling.
  */
